1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,549
Before her agent can buy anything new, she reads exactly what she signs.

2
00:00:09,599 --> 00:00:20,616
This is Countersign. A receivables fund buys payments that companies will receive later, and investors buy its units. Our agent buys those units for her.

3
00:00:20,666 --> 00:00:30,816
This competition's banking brief puts investment subscription in the red tier. The hard part for an agent is not talking about an investment. It is buying one.

4
00:00:30,866 --> 00:00:43,383
Our composite persona, built from public data and not validated by interviews, keeps savings in USDC. She gives up at one screen: the confirmation of her first purchase.

5
00:00:43,433 --> 00:00:53,883
Agents have already lost money through a chat, through an operator panel, and through a signing screen that showed one thing while the transaction did another.

6
00:00:53,933 --> 00:01:06,016
So we built an investing agent that cannot buy a fund unit unless the investor's mandate, the fund's issuer and a wallet contract the agent cannot change agree.

7
00:01:06,066 --> 00:01:20,149
The agent's wallet is a contract on Stellar: it can only pay fund wallets the investor approved, within limits she signed, and it cannot change those limits. Nobody holds a fund unit unless the fund's issuer signs the mint.

8
00:01:20,199 --> 00:01:34,016
She states her goal in one sentence. The agent asks only what is missing and fills a typed mandate. Nothing is valid until she approves it.

9
00:01:34,066 --> 00:01:52,916
Her mandate wallet is our wallet contract on Stellar, not audited. She deploys it with her caps, signs it in her own wallet, and deposits test USDC.

10
00:01:52,966 --> 00:02:08,916
She asks for a purchase, then changes her mind. The plan is redone without starting over.

11
00:02:08,966 --> 00:02:20,916
A first purchase in a new fund waits for her signature, on a card decoded from the transaction bytes, not written by the model.

12
00:02:20,966 --> 00:02:32,916
The agent executes within the mandate she signed. Our policy approved it, and the hash of that decision travels with the payment.

13
00:02:32,966 --> 00:02:48,516
Anyone can open the transaction and see the decision on chain: the decision argument is the hash of log line twenty, the line of our log that approved this purchase.

14
00:02:48,566 --> 00:03:09,283
Nobody holds a fund unit until the fund's issuer countersigns the mint. The fund manager approves the order and signs in her own wallet; the units are minted on chain, and the investor's screen shows the purchase completed.

15
00:03:09,333 --> 00:03:20,116
Recurring purchases are pre-authorised within her mandate, and she can interrupt them. The time trigger is simulated; the transactions are real.

16
00:03:20,166 --> 00:03:37,950
From our test suite: twelve adversarial cases, all blocked or recovered. Four were refused on chain by our wallet contract, even with the agent key in hand.

17
00:03:38,000 --> 00:04:05,950
The issuer can freeze the units. She can revoke the agent with one signature; our wallet contract on Stellar, not audited, then refuses its purchases: the next recurring purchase was refused before anything was built or signed.

18
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:23,050
Eleven of twelve scripted tasks completed, in about one turn each. Every hash has a task and a log line.

19
00:04:23,100 --> 00:04:32,250
Not offered in Mainland China; we name the February 2026 notice. No licence, simulated KYC, test assets.

20
00:04:32,300 --> 00:04:49,416
These are our limitations, the same in every document: our contracts are not audited, the checks are simulated, and every asset is a test asset. Please read them before anything else we showed.

21
00:04:49,466 --> 00:04:56,950
Countersign. Sign only what you can read. Reviewers: live demo accounts are in our submission form.

